<html>
<head>
	<meta content="text/html; charset=utf-8"/>
	<link rel="stylesheet" href=":/resources/cheatsheet/cheatsheet.css" type="text/css" />
</head>

<body>
	<h1>Segmentation Shape Interpolation</h1>

	<p><a href=#annotation_seg>Segmentation table view</a> | <a href=#pref_seg>Visibility preferences</a></p>
	<p><a href=#navi>Navigation preferences</a></p>

	<p>
	Paint an object on a few spaced-out slices; everything between them is filled in and
	previewed as you go. Enter commits the whole chain. Key slices are amber, interpolated
	ones cyan, and the status bar shows the plane and slice count.
	</p>

	<p>
	The axis comes from the viewport you paint the first slice in — <b>xy</b> interpolates
	along z, <b>xz</b> along y, <b>zy</b> along x — and the other two show a cross-section
	through the chain. A chain belongs to one axis; with at most one key slice, painting
	elsewhere moves it, otherwise press Esc first. Painted slices are always kept.
	</p>

	<p>
	Left click an existing slice to make it the key slice; right click adds a piece to it, so
	a label split into separate parts in one plane — the two arms of a U above the join — takes
	one right click each. Either way the whole outline is picked up, not just the part on screen. Paint or erase on an interpolated slice to correct it, and only
	the part you touch changes.
	</p>

	<p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th align="left">Shortcut</th><th align="left">Action</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Shift + <img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/leftclick.png" alt="Left click"/> drag</span></td><td>Draw — the only painting gesture in this mode</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Shift + <img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/rightclick.png" alt="Right click"/> drag</span></td><td>Erase</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Shift + <img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/middleclick.png" alt="Middle click"/> scroll</span></td><td>Change brush size</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh"><img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/middleclick.png" alt="Middle click"/> click</span></td><td>2D fill (stops at the edge of the loaded blocks)</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Shift + <img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/middleclick.png" alt="Middle click"/> click</span></td><td>3D fill (always confined to the loaded blocks)</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">G</span></td><td>2D fill at the crosshair</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Shift + G</span></td><td>3D fill at the crosshair</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Enter</span></td><td>Accept — write the interpolated volume</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Ctrl + P</span></td><td>Toggle interpolation preview</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Esc</span></td><td>End the current chain (painted slices are kept)</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">S</span></td><td>Toggle between Segmentation Paint and Shape Interpolation</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">&larr;</span></td><td>Jump to previous painted slice</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">&rarr;</span></td><td>Jump to next painted slice</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Shift + &larr;</span></td><td>Jump to first painted slice</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Shift + &rarr;</span></td><td>Jump to last painted slice</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Delete</span> / <span class="sh">Backspace</span></td><td>Delete the painted slice at the current depth</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">D</span> / <span class="sh">F</span></td><td>Step one slice back / forward</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">overwrite</span> (toolbar)</td><td>What the brush may write over: everything, only unlabelled voxels, or only unlabelled voxels not touching another label — which keeps a one-voxel gap around every object</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Action menu</span></td><td>Lock painting to the current magnification</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">C</span> / <span class="sh">N</span></td><td>New segmentation object (id above the layer’s max id)</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">0</span> / <span class="sh">id</span> (toolbar) / <img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/leftclick.png" alt="Left click"/> on empty space outside the preview</td><td>Paint the background instead of a label — the brush and both fills then erase, so a fill rubs out the connected chunk it lands on and the key slice is re-read to match. The id box turns red. Click the chain’s object, or one of its key slices, to go back</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Ctrl + Z</span></td><td>Undo the last segmentation edit</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Ctrl + Shift + Z</span></td><td>Redo</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">-</span></td><td>Overlay: decrease opacity</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">+</span></td><td>Overlay: increase opacity</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Hold space</span></td><td>Reveal raw data temporarily</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh"><img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/leftclick.png" alt="Left click"/></span></td><td>Select object; on this chain’s object, <b>replace</b> the key slice with that piece</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh"><img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/rightclick.png" alt="Right click"/></span></td><td><b>Add</b> that piece to the key slice — click each part of a split label in turn</td></tr>
		<tr><td><span class="sh">Ctrl + <img src=":/resources/cheatsheet/leftclick.png" alt="Left click"/></span></td><td>Take another object’s outline in this slice: relabels those voxels to the current id and adopts them</td></tr>
	</table>
	</p>
</body>
</html>
